  "reasoning": "The patient presents with persistent shivering, feeling cold, and a very high fever, pointing to a systemic infection with pronounced chills. The additional symptoms of rashes on the arms and red patches on the neck indicate a condition that features both febrile episodes and cutaneous manifestations. Malaria typically causes high fever and chills but rarely includes rashes. Chicken pox involves fever and rash but is distinguished by vesicular lesions rather than simple red patches, and drug reactions do not typically present with the prominent infectious chills described. Dengue fever classically presents with sudden high fever, severe chills/shivering, body aches, and a characteristic maculopapular rash that often begins on the extremities (arms) and spreads to the trunk and neck. The precise combination of high fever, marked shivering, and localized red rashes strongly establishes dengue as the correct diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
